Here is a TypeScript function that computes the median of an array of numbers.

The median is the middle value of a sorted dataset. If the dataset has an odd number of elements, the median is the middle element. If it has an even number of elements, the median is the average of the two middle elements.

```typescript
/**
 * Computes the median of an array of numbers.
 *
 * @param numbers An array of numbers.
 * @returns The median of the array, or null if the array is empty.
 */
function findMedian(numbers: number[]): number | null {
    if (numbers.length === 0) {
        return null;
    }

    // 1. Sort the array numerically
    const sortedNumbers: number[] = [...numbers].sort((a, b) => a - b);

    const length: number = sortedNumbers.length;
    const middleIndex: number = Math.floor(length / 2);

    if (length % 2 === 1) {
        // Odd number of elements: return the middle element
        return sortedNumbers[middleIndex];
    } else {
        // Even number of elements: return the average of the two middle elements
        const lowerMiddle = sortedNumbers[middleIndex - 1];
        const upperMiddle = sortedNumbers[middleIndex];
        return (lowerMiddle + upperMiddle) / 2;
    }
}

// --- Examples ---

// Example 1: Odd number of elements
const oddArray: number[] = [5, 2, 9, 1, 7];
// Sorted: [1, 2, 5, 7, 9]. Median is 5.
const medianOdd = findMedian(oddArray);
console.log(`Median of [${oddArray}]: ${medianOdd}`); // Output: 5

// Example 2: Even number of elements
const evenArray: number[] = [10, 4, 8, 2];
// Sorted: [2, 4, 8, 10]. Median is (4 + 8) / 2 = 6.
const medianEven = findMedian(evenArray);
console.log(`Median of [${evenArray}]: ${medianEven}`); // Output: 6

// Example 3: Array with duplicates and negative numbers
const mixedArray: number[] = [-5, 10, 0, 10, 3];
// Sorted: [-5, 0, 3, 10, 10]. Median is 3.
const medianMixed = findMedian(mixedArray);
console.log(`Median of [${mixedArray}]: ${medianMixed}`); // Output: 3

// Example 4: Empty array
const emptyArray: number[] = [];
const medianEmpty = findMedian(emptyArray);
console.log(`Median of []: ${medianEmpty}`); // Output: null
```

Here is a TypeScript class for a stack implementation. I've used a standard JavaScript array internally to store the stack elements.

```typescript
/**
 * A class representing a Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) stack.
 */
class Stack<T> {
    /**
     * The internal array used to store the stack elements.
     * @private
     */
    private items: T[];

    /**
     * Creates an instance of Stack.
     */
    constructor() {
        this.items = [];
    }

    /**
     * Adds an element to the top of the stack.
     * @param {T} element The element to be added to the stack.
     */
    public push(element: T): void {
        this.items.push(element);
        console.log(`Pushed: ${element}`);
    }

    /**
     * Removes and returns the element at the top of the stack.
     * Returns undefined if the stack is empty.
     * @returns {T | undefined} The element removed from the stack, or undefined if the stack is empty.
     */
    public pop(): T | undefined {
        if (this.isEmpty()) {
            console.warn("Cannot pop from an empty stack.");
            return undefined;
        }
        const poppedItem = this.items.pop();
        console.log(`Popped: ${poppedItem}`);
        return poppedItem;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the element at the top of the stack without removing it.
     * Returns undefined if the stack is empty.
     * @returns {T | undefined} The element at the top of the stack, or undefined if the stack is empty.
     */
    public peek(): T | undefined {
        if (this.isEmpty()) {
            console.warn("Cannot peek at an empty stack.");
            return undefined;
        }
        // The top element is the last element in the array
        return this.items[this.items.length - 1];
    }

    /**
     * Checks if the stack is empty.
     * @returns {boolean} True if the stack contains no elements, false otherwise.
     */
    public isEmpty(): boolean {
        return this.items.length === 0;
    }

    /**
     * Returns the current number of elements in the stack.
     * @returns {number} The size of the stack.
     */
    public size(): number {
        return this.items.length;
    }
}

// --- Example Usage ---
console.log("--- Creating a Stack of Numbers ---");
const numberStack = new Stack<number>();

numberStack.push(10);
numberStack.push(20);
numberStack.push(30);

console.log(`Current size: ${numberStack.size()}`);
console.log(`Top element (peek): ${numberStack.peek()}`); // Should be 30

const poppedValue1 = numberStack.pop();
console.log(`Popped value: ${poppedValue1}`); // Should be 30

console.log(`New top element (peek): ${numberStack.peek()}`); // Should be 20

numberStack.pop();
numberStack.pop();

console.log(`Is the stack empty? ${numberStack.isEmpty()}`); // Should be true

